//? Title: Least-Squares GAN: Raw Scores and Explicit Targets
//? Subtitle: Complete native MNIST experiment, separate Adam states and fixed final epoch
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A compact adversarial game fits 1000 balanced MNIST digits, with 200 validation and
200 held-out test images. The generator maps 16 Gaussian coordinates through 32 tanh
units to 784 sigmoid pixels; the discriminator produces one raw source score.
Digit labels do not enter either network or its objective.
Three fixed epochs perform 96 discriminator writes and 96 generator writes, always
recomputing the generator loss through the updated discriminator. Canonical C++ maps
and objectives supply the model; the complete editable program exposes every update.
The short run produces weak grey images. Game losses and coarse-pixel MMD remain
diagnostics, and the fitting-mean baseline scores better on MMD. Exact source parity,
independent numerical checks, checkpoint restore and noise controls preserve this result.
)
\toc

> Follow the supplied inputs through the game

The architecture and objective follow the repository's compact teaching experiment.
The method reference is [Mao et al., Least Squares Generative Adversarial Networks (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.04076). The discriminator receives an image alone. A source target enters its loss, not its forward input.
No sigmoid turns the discriminator score into a probability.


```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lsgan_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
