> Changed noise is a control, not evidence of realistic samples

Seed4877 supplies the changed bank; the zero bank has the same shape. The restored G
responds to both changes, while standalone generation on the fixed bank matches the
full forward bit for bit. The first sample agrees between single and batch inference
(maximum error0). Every saved weight restores exactly, and all diagnostic calls leave
both stores unchanged. Responsiveness proves dependence on the input, not useful mode
coverage, diversity of recognizable digits or convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lsgan_noise.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> a, c;
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("build/lsgan/outputs.npz"))
    a.emplace(k, v);
  for (const auto& [k, v] : n::load_npz("build/lsgan/controls.npz"))
    c.emplace(k, v);
  auto first  = [](const auto& x) { return n::slice_axis(x, 0, 0, 8); };
  auto images = n::concat(
      {first(a.at("fake")), first(c.at("changed_images")), first(c.at("zero_images"))}, 0);
  n::save_image_grid(
      images.reshape({24, 28, 28, 1}), "build/lsgan_noise.png", 8, 0, 1, 4, 220 / 255., 3);
  std::print(
      "Rows: fixed seed4831; changed seed4877; zero input. All control weights unchanged.\n");
}
```
